AuthClaw — Board Briefing                                                                       AgentsArchitects.ai

1. Executive Summary

Enterprises are adopting artificial intelligence faster than they can govern it. Every time a company
sends data to an AI model, it risks leaking sensitive customer or patient information, breaching
regulations such as GDPR or HIPAA, and failing the audits that large customers now demand before
they will buy. Today, most companies manage this risk with manual reviews, spreadsheets, and
hope.

AuthClaw closes that gap. It is a software product that sits between a company's applications and
the AI models they use, acting as an automated safety checkpoint. It removes sensitive data in real
time, enforces the company's rules, fixes compliance problems with human sign-off, and keeps a
tamper-proof record that can be handed straight to an auditor.

This briefing explains — in non-technical terms — what AuthClaw is, why the timing is right, how
large the opportunity is, who buys it, what it will cost to build, and the decision we are asking the
Board to approve.

  The ask, in one line
  Approve a four-stage build of the AuthClaw product — roughly 2,560 engineering hours (an
  indicative engineering investment of about US$123,000) — to reach a sellable, audit-ready first
  version.
   Third-party costs (security testing, SOC 2 audit) and go-to-market are additional and are flagged separately
   in Section 10.

2. The Problem We Are Solving

Three forces are colliding inside almost every mid-to-large company right now:

2.1 AI is being adopted everywhere, quickly
Teams across customer support, finance, legal, and operations are wiring AI models into their daily
work. Most of this happens without a central safety layer, so the company often cannot say exactly
what data is being sent to which AI provider.

2.2 Regulators are raising the stakes
Data-protection laws (GDPR, HIPAA) and security standards (SOC 2) already carry real penalties.
The EU's new AI Act goes further: non-compliance can cost up to €35 million or 7% of global
annual turnover, whichever is higher. For a large enterprise, that is a board-level financial risk, not
an IT footnote.

2.3 Customers now audit their suppliers
Large buyers increasingly refuse to sign contracts until a supplier can prove its security and
compliance posture. Today that proof is assembled by hand for every deal, slowing sales and tying
up senior staff.

The cost of doing nothing
Sensitive data can leak to external AI providers without anyone noticing until it is too late.

