                                                                                           one trustworthy report.
                           Every action, decision, and approval is written into a tamper-
                           proof record that cannot be secretly altered. This can be
                           exported as verifiable proof for auditors and customers.

The safeguard that matters most to a Board

AuthClaw can recommend and prepare fixes, but it cannot execute anything risky without an explicit human
approval backed by a security check, and that approval automatically expires if not acted on quickly.
In short: the machine does the heavy lifting; a person always stays in control of consequential decisions.

4. Why Now

AuthClaw is not an idea ahead of its market. The timing is being driven by forces outside our control,
all pointing the same way:

   • Regulation has teeth. The EU AI Act began phasing in through 2025, with severe penalties.
       Other regions are following. Compliance is becoming a mandatory budget line, not an optional
       one.

   • AI adoption is mainstream. The question for enterprises is no longer whether to use AI, but
       how to use it safely — exactly the gap AuthClaw fills.

   • Buyers are paying for trust. Insurers are now linking premiums to certified governance, and
       customers gate deals on proof of compliance. Trust has become a purchasing criterion.

   • The category is funded and proven. Established companies already sell adjacent pieces of
       this puzzle (see Section 6), which de-risks the market: we are entering a validated category,
       not inventing one.

